from extras import ex

ex('particles',"The particle model explains properties. Solids keep their shape because particles are held in place. Gases can be squashed because there are big spaces between particles.",
 [('Why can a gas be compressed?','There are large spaces between its particles.','Its particles are very small.','Its particles do not move.','Pushing moves particles into the gaps.'),
  ('Why does a liquid take the shape of its container?','Its particles can move past each other.','Its particles are far apart.','Its particles vibrate in fixed positions.','Particles slide around to fill the bottom of the container.'),
  ('In which state are the particles most tightly packed?','Solid.','Liquid.','Gas.','Solid particles are close together in a regular pattern.'),
  ('When a solid is heated, its particles…','vibrate faster.','get bigger.','stop moving.','Heating gives particles more energy, not more size.')],
 [('Describe how particles are arranged in a gas.','Far apart and randomly arranged / moving quickly in all directions.'),
  ('Use the particle model to explain why solids cannot flow.','Particles are held in fixed positions.~They can only vibrate, so they cannot move past each other.'),
  ('Explain why a syringe full of air can be pushed in, but a syringe full of water cannot.','Air is a gas with large spaces between particles.~Pushing moves gas particles closer together.~Water particles are already touching, so there is no space to squash them into.')])

ex('state',"The melting point is the temperature at which a solid melts. A pure substance melts and boils at fixed temperatures. Heating gives particles more energy to break away from each other.",
 [('What is condensing?','A gas turning into a liquid.','A liquid turning into a gas.','A solid turning into a liquid.','Water vapour condensing makes droplets on a cold window.'),
  ('At what temperature does pure water boil at sea level?','100 °C.','0 °C.','50 °C.','Pure water boils at 100 °C at normal pressure.'),
  ('A puddle dries up on a warm day. This is…','evaporation.','boiling.','freezing.','Evaporation happens at the surface below the boiling point.'),
  ('During melting, the temperature of ice…','stays the same until it has all melted.','keeps rising steadily.','falls.','Energy is used to break the solid apart.')],
 [('Name the change of state from liquid to solid.','Freezing.'),
  ('Describe two differences between evaporation and boiling.','Evaporation only happens at the surface; boiling happens throughout the liquid.~Evaporation happens at any temperature; boiling happens at the boiling point.'),
  ('Explain, using particles, what happens when ice melts.','Heating transfers energy to the particles.~The particles vibrate more strongly.~They break free of their fixed positions and can move past each other.')])

ex('gas',"Heating a gas makes its particles move faster. They hit the walls harder and more often, so pressure rises. Air pressure acts on us all the time because air particles hit us from every direction.",
 [('What happens to gas pressure if the gas is heated in a sealed can?','It increases.','It decreases.','It stays the same.','Faster particles hit the walls harder and more often.'),
  ('What causes gas pressure?','Particles hitting the walls of the container.','Particles getting bigger.','Particles sticking together.','Each collision pushes on the wall.'),
  ('A balloon is squashed to half its size. The pressure inside…','increases.','decreases.','becomes zero.','Particles hit the walls more often in a smaller space.'),
  ('Why does a crisp packet puff up at the top of a mountain?','Air pressure outside is lower.','The air inside gets colder.','The crisps expand.','Less pressure outside lets the gas inside push outwards.')],
 [('What causes the pressure of a gas?','Gas particles colliding with the walls of the container.'),
  ('Explain why you should not heat a sealed can.','The gas particles move faster and hit the walls harder/more often.~The pressure could rise enough to burst the can.'),
  ('A football is pumped up with more air. Explain why it feels harder.','More particles are inside the same space.~They hit the inside walls more often.~So the pressure increases.')])

ex('atoms',"Chemical formulae show which atoms are joined, for example H₂O has two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. A compound's properties can be very different from its elements. Mixtures can be separated by physical methods.",
 [('Which is a compound?','Water, H₂O.','Oxygen, O₂.','Iron, Fe.','Water has two different elements chemically joined.'),
  ('Air is…','a mixture.','an element.','a compound.','Air contains different gases that are not joined.'),
  ('How many atoms are in CO₂?','3.','2.','1.','One carbon atom and two oxygen atoms.'),
  ('Sodium is a reactive metal and chlorine is a toxic gas. Sodium chloride is…','table salt, which is safe to eat in small amounts.','also a toxic gas.','a mixture of the two.','A compound can have very different properties from its elements.')],
 [('What is an element?','A substance made of only one type of atom.'),
  ('State the number of each type of atom in H₂SO₄.','2 hydrogen and 1 sulfur.~4 oxygen.'),
  ('Explain the difference between a mixture of iron and sulfur and the compound iron sulfide.','In the mixture the atoms are not chemically joined.~The mixture can be separated, e.g. with a magnet.~In iron sulfide they are chemically joined, so a magnet cannot separate them.')])

ex('solution',"Particles of the solute spread out between solvent particles. The mass is conserved: mass of solute + mass of solvent = mass of solution. Most solids dissolve better in warmer water.",
 [('20 g of salt dissolves in 100 g of water. What is the mass of the solution?','120 g.','100 g.','80 g.','Mass is conserved when dissolving.'),
  ('In salty water, the solvent is…','water.','salt.','salty water.','The solvent is the liquid that dissolves the solute.'),
  ('Which usually lets more sugar dissolve?','Warmer water.','Colder water.','Less stirring.','Solubility of most solids increases with temperature.'),
  ('A substance that does not dissolve is…','insoluble.','soluble.','saturated.','Sand is insoluble in water.')],
 [('What is a solute?','The substance that dissolves.'),
  ('15 g of sugar is dissolved in 85 g of water. Calculate the mass of the solution and explain your answer.','15 + 85 = 100 g.~No particles are lost when dissolving, so mass is conserved.'),
  ('Explain what happens to the particles when sugar dissolves in water.','The sugar particles separate from each other.~Water particles surround them.~The sugar particles spread out between the water particles, so they are too small to see.')])

ex('filter',"Evaporating all the water quickly leaves small crystals. Letting it evaporate slowly makes larger crystals. Rock salt can be purified by dissolving, filtering and then evaporating.",
 [('What is left on the filter paper?','The residue.','The filtrate.','The solvent.','The insoluble solid is trapped as residue.'),
  ('Which mixture can be separated by filtering?','Sand and water.','Salt and water.','Ink and water.','Sand is insoluble, so it is trapped.'),
  ('Slow evaporation gives…','larger crystals.','smaller crystals.','no crystals.','Crystals have more time to grow.'),
  ('Why is rock salt dissolved in water before filtering?','To separate the soluble salt from insoluble sand and grit.','To make the salt heavier.','To melt the rock.','Only dissolved salt passes through the paper.')],
 [('What is the filtrate?','The liquid that passes through the filter paper.'),
  ('Describe how to get dry sand from a mixture of sand and water.','Filter the mixture.~Leave the sand (residue) to dry / dry it in a warm place.'),
  ('Explain how to get pure salt crystals from rock salt.','Dissolve the rock salt in warm water and stir.~Filter to remove the insoluble sand and grit.~Evaporate some water and leave the filtrate to crystallise.')])

ex('distil',"Pure water can be made from sea water by distillation. In chromatography, the solvent moves up the paper and carries dissolved dyes different distances. Draw the start line in pencil, because pencil does not dissolve.",
 [('Which method gets pure water from sea water?','Distillation.','Filtration.','Chromatography.','Water evaporates and condenses; salt stays behind.'),
  ('Why is the start line drawn in pencil in chromatography?','Pencil does not dissolve in the solvent.','Pencil is easier to rub out.','Ink is too expensive.','An ink line would move with the solvent.'),
  ('What does the condenser do?','Cools vapour back into a liquid.','Heats the mixture.','Filters the liquid.','Cold water around it cools the vapour.'),
  ('A black ink separates into three spots. This shows that the ink…','is a mixture of at least three dyes.','is a pure substance.','is insoluble.','Each spot is a different dye.')],
 [('What does chromatography separate?','Dissolved substances, such as dyes in an ink.'),
  ('Describe how distillation separates water from salty water.','Heat the salty water so the water evaporates.~The vapour is cooled in a condenser and collected as pure water.'),
  ('Explain why the dyes in an ink move different distances up the paper.','The solvent moves up the paper, carrying the dyes.~Different dyes dissolve in the solvent and stick to the paper by different amounts.~Dyes that are more soluble move further.')])

ex('energy',"Other energy stores include gravitational potential (raised objects), elastic (stretched springs) and thermal (hot objects). A falling ball transfers energy from its gravitational store to its kinetic store. Some energy is usually wasted by heating the surroundings.",
 [('A stretched spring has energy in which store?','Elastic potential.','Kinetic.','Chemical.','Stretching or squashing stores elastic energy.'),
  ('As a ball falls, energy transfers from…','gravitational potential to kinetic.','kinetic to chemical.','elastic to nuclear.','It loses height and gains speed.'),
  ('A torch transfers energy from a battery to…','light and the thermal store of the surroundings.','the battery’s kinetic store.','nuclear energy.','Light is useful; heating is wasted.'),
  ('Energy cannot be…','created or destroyed.','transferred.','stored.','This is the law of conservation of energy.')],
 [('Name the energy store of a hot cup of tea.','Thermal store.'),
  ('Describe the energy transfer when a car brakes to a stop.','The kinetic store of the car decreases.~Energy is transferred to the thermal store of the brakes/surroundings.'),
  ('A ball is dropped and bounces lower each time. Explain where the energy goes.','Energy is not destroyed.~Some is transferred to the thermal store of the ball, floor and air.~Some is carried away by sound, so less is left for the next bounce.')])

ex('food-energy',"Food labels show energy in kilojoules (kJ). An active teenager needs more energy each day than someone who is less active. Eating more energy than you use is stored, often as fat.",
 [('Food energy on labels is usually given in…','kilojoules (kJ).','newtons (N).','watts (W).','1 kJ = 1000 J.'),
  ('Who needs the most energy each day?','An active teenager who plays lots of sport.','A sleeping adult.','A small child resting.','Activity and growth need more energy.'),
  ('Why compare foods using the same mass?','To make the test fair.','To make the food burn.','To change the dependent variable.','Different masses would hold different amounts of energy.'),
  ('In the burning food test, why might results be too low?','Energy heats the air and equipment, not just the water.','The water is too clean.','The food has too much energy.','Not all energy reaches the water.')],
 [('In which store is energy kept in food?','Chemical store.'),
  ('A crisp raises the water temperature by 15 °C and a peanut by 25 °C. Which transferred more energy, and how can you tell?','The peanut.~It caused a bigger temperature rise in the same water.'),
  ('Explain one way to improve the burning-food experiment.','Use the same mass of each food / insulate the tube / keep the food close to the tube.~This reduces energy lost to the surroundings or makes the test fair.~So the temperature rise better represents the energy in the food.')])

ex('resources',"Non-renewable resources include coal, oil, gas and nuclear fuel. Renewable resources include wind, solar, hydroelectric, tidal and biomass. Each resource has advantages and disadvantages, such as cost, reliability and pollution.",
 [('Which is a renewable energy resource?','Solar power.','Coal.','Natural gas.','Sunlight will not run out.'),
  ('Why are fossil fuels non-renewable?','They take millions of years to form.','They are found underground.','They are expensive.','We use them much faster than they form.'),
  ('A disadvantage of wind power is that…','it does not work when the wind is not blowing.','it releases carbon dioxide.','it will run out.','Supply depends on the weather.'),
  ('Burning fossil fuels adds which gas to the air?','Carbon dioxide.','Oxygen.','Nitrogen.','Carbon dioxide adds to the greenhouse effect.')],
 [('Name one non-renewable energy resource.','Coal / oil / natural gas / nuclear fuel.'),
  ('Give one advantage and one disadvantage of solar panels.','Advantage: renewable / no carbon dioxide while running.~Disadvantage: no electricity at night / less on cloudy days / takes up space.'),
  ('Explain why many countries are building more wind and solar farms.','Fossil fuels will run out.~Burning them releases carbon dioxide, which adds to climate change.~Wind and solar are renewable and do not release carbon dioxide when generating electricity.')])

ex('power',"Devices with a higher power rating use energy faster. A 3 kW kettle transfers energy faster than a 0.1 kW TV. You can save money by using lower-power devices or using devices for less time.",
 [('A 2 kW heater is on for 3 hours. How much energy does it use?','6 kWh.','5 kWh.','1.5 kWh.','2 × 3 = 6 kWh.'),
  ('Energy costs 30p per kWh. What does 4 kWh cost?','120p (£1.20).','34p.','7.5p.','4 × 30 = 120p.'),
  ('Which device has the highest power?','A 3 kW kettle.','A 100 W TV.','A 10 W phone charger.','3 kW = 3000 W.'),
  ('What is 500 W in kilowatts?','0.5 kW.','5 kW.','50 kW.','Divide by 1000.')],
 [('What unit is power measured in?','Watts (W).'),
  ('A 1.5 kW microwave is used for 2 hours. Electricity costs 25p per kWh. Calculate the cost.','Energy = 1.5 × 2 = 3 kWh.~Cost = 3 × 25 = 75p.'),
  ('Suggest and explain two ways a family could reduce their electricity bill.','Use lower-power appliances, e.g. LED bulbs.~Use appliances for less time / switch off standby.~Both reduce the number of kWh used, so the cost is lower.')])

ex('efficiency',"Wasted energy usually heats the surroundings. LED bulbs are more efficient than filament bulbs because they waste less energy as heat. Insulation and lubrication can reduce wasted energy.",
 [('A bulb transfers 100 J; 20 J is light. What is its efficiency?','20%.','80%.','120%.','20 ÷ 100 × 100 = 20%.'),
  ('Where does most wasted energy end up?','The thermal store of the surroundings.','Back in the battery.','It disappears.','Energy spreads out as heat.'),
  ('Which bulb is more efficient?','An LED bulb.','A filament bulb.','They are always equal.','LEDs waste less energy by heating.'),
  ('Oil on a bike chain improves efficiency by…','reducing friction.','adding energy.','making it heavier.','Less friction means less wasted heating.')],
 [('What does efficiency tell you?','The fraction/percentage of energy transferred usefully.'),
  ('A motor uses 500 J and transfers 400 J usefully. Calculate its efficiency.','400 ÷ 500 = 0.8.~0.8 × 100 = 80%.'),
  ('Explain why no device can be 100% efficient.','Energy is always transferred to unwanted stores.~Friction and electrical resistance heat the device and surroundings.~So the useful energy out is always less than the energy in.')])

ex('variation',"Some features depend on both genes and environment, such as height and weight. Variation within a species can be shown on a graph. Continuous variation makes a bell-shaped curve, while discontinuous variation is shown on a bar chart.",
 [('Which is discontinuous variation?','Blood group.','Height.','Mass.','Blood group has only a few separate types.'),
  ('Which feature is caused only by the environment?','A scar.','Natural eye colour.','Blood group.','A scar comes from an injury, not genes.'),
  ('Which graph suits continuous variation?','A histogram or line graph showing a range.','A pie chart only.','A bar chart of categories.','Continuous data has values across a range.'),
  ('Identical twins have different weights. This is mainly due to…','the environment, such as diet and exercise.','different genes.','different blood groups.','Identical twins share the same genes.')],
 [('What is variation?','Differences between living things of the same species.'),
  ('Give one example of continuous and one of discontinuous variation in humans.','Continuous: height / mass / hand span.~Discontinuous: blood group / tongue rolling / natural eye colour.'),
  ('Explain why identical twins can still look different as adults.','Identical twins have the same genes.~Differences come from their environment.~For example, diet, exercise, sun exposure or injuries.')])

ex('adaptation',"Animals in cold places often have thick fur, fat and small ears to reduce heat loss. Desert plants have small leaves or spines and deep roots. Predators often have forward-facing eyes; prey animals often have eyes on the sides of their heads.",
 [('Why does a polar bear have small ears?','To reduce heat loss.','To hear better.','To swim faster.','Small ears have less surface area to lose heat.'),
  ('Why does a cactus have spines instead of leaves?','To reduce water loss.','To catch insects.','To make more food.','Smaller surface area loses less water.'),
  ('Prey animals often have eyes on the sides of their heads to…','see predators coming from many directions.','judge distance precisely.','see in the dark.','A wide field of view helps them spot danger.'),
  ('Which is a behavioural adaptation?','Birds migrating to warmer places in winter.','Thick fur.','Sharp teeth.','Behaviour is something the animal does.')],
 [('What is an adaptation?','A feature that helps a living thing survive and reproduce in its environment.'),
  ('Give two adaptations of a camel to life in the desert.','Hump stores fat / wide feet for walking on sand.~Long eyelashes keep out sand / loses little water.'),
  ('Explain how a thick layer of fat helps a seal survive in cold water.','Fat is a good insulator.~It reduces energy transfer from the body to the cold water.~So the seal keeps warm and can survive.')])
